@ 10.3.11.1 - Proto-Southern Vanuatu to Anejom
# citation: thetha, from Lynch, John (2001), The Linguistic History of Southern Vanuatu “a lot of word medial vowels get elided, sometimes even when they should be stressed”
	C > ∅ / _# // C = t
	t > s / _#
	vʷ > h
	k > ∅ / V_V (sporadic?)
	{ bʷ, ɡ } > { pʷ, k }
	s > h / rarely
	s > θ / , occasionally // _i
	{ d, {c,*j} } > { t͡ʃ, s }
	{n,ŋ} > ɲ / _:[+front]
	w > v
	l > t͡ʃ / :{ _o, _:[+front] }:
	q > ∅
	V > ∅ / _# (with very few exceptions)
	{r,h} > ∅ / _#
	{i,o} > e
	u > o
	i > o / :{ u_, w_ }:
	u > e / :{ θ_, ɣ_ }:
	u > e / _θ
	ai > i / _C
	ei > i
	ua > ou
	au > {u,o} “sometimes”
	e > i / Ḱ_ :[tendency]
	e > i / _Ḱ :[tendency]
	a > o / P_ :[tendency]
	a > o / _P :[tendency]